WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.560
When several pull requests are waiting,
I need to decide where to start.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.240
I'll open Triage, narrow the queue
to the work that needs my action,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.500
and read the reason
behind a recommendation.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.780
Then I'll use the card to
open a detailed review.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.000
We'll also look at the Slack digest,
which brings the queue into the

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.100
team's conversation and gives me a
place to pick up the next review.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.170
I open Triage to see pull requests across

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.210
the repositories available to this account.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.950
I'm looking for our
checkout and invoice change.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.780
I check the repository,
pull request number,

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:38.710
and title before opening it, so I
know I'm following the right example.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.210
This change introduces promotional
pricing and invoice features.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.490
We already have two
questions to investigate.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:48.590
Whether customers can see
only their own invoices and

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.170
whether the discount applies
at the correct quantity.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.170
The queue gives me a view of
the work waiting for attention.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.610
I'll narrow it to my responsibilities
before deciding where to begin.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.870
I select Requires my action,
which brings together

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.870
pull requests involving me
as an author or reviewer.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.830
For this part of the demo, I'm
using Mike's reviewer account.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.830
I search for the
demonstration repository and

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:15.010
find the checkout and invoice pull request.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.050
As the reviewer, my job is to examine the

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.370
proposed behavior and
the response to feedback.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:26.550
If the code needs to change, the author
can prepare an update for another review.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.370
The search keeps this example in view.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.210
I can return to All PRs whenever
I need to see the wider queue.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.370
I open View priority
reason from the card to

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.430
see why this pull request needs attention.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:47.250
This example is P2, Medium priority,
and it also shows High security risk.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.790
Those labels describe different things.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.250
Queue priority helps me
compare work while the security

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.430
risk tells me about a
concern within this change.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.290
The explanation points
to security issues and

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.710
problems with the data the change returns.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.770
I can use that reasoning to
decide what to inspect first.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.590
Here I'd start with invoice search because

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.570
it could expose another customer's data.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:18.210
The discount boundary also matters and
we'll inspect that as we review the code.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.590
I now have a reason for choosing this pull

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.810
request and a specific
question to investigate.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.370
I return to the card and
open its action menu.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.410
The available actions sit
alongside the recommendations

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.730
so I can see how to move the work forward.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.170
I also check its current status.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.330
This card shows passed checks
and requests for changes.

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.150
Passing checks are useful
but the review still

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.230
raises questions about the
application's behavior.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.290
In this example, I want to read the code
and findings before requesting a repair.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.730
I follow the Change Stack link
to open the detailed review.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.150
That takes me from choosing a pull request
to investigating the change itself.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.930
The queue can also reach me in Slack.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:04.270
Here is a digest from
the author's connected

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.950
account with examples
from other repositories.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.250
The message groups work into Now and Next.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.810
Each card identifies the
repository and pull request,

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.530
summarizes the change and shows its status.

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:20.030
I scroll through the available actions.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.810
One card offers Merge and Find reviewer,
another offers Fix merge conflicts.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.910
Those give the team a starting
point for the next step.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.550
We still check the review evidence
before accepting a change.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.390
I return to Mike's reviewer account and
our checkout and invoice pull request.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.250
From this card, we'll continue to
Change Stack to examine the code.
